118|null@relationships^1438913733#3g2ylc:solonosolo$:My [16M] mom [40F] just told me she's pregnant. I am worried that the new baby will replace me.
1|0@relationships^1438957258#ctusmo1:BeefGriller$:You have changes in your life - it's normal to feel some anxiety over them. As a father to three children, I can tell you that there is as much love in my heart for the oldest as there is for the youngest. That's a funny thing about parental love - when there are more children, the love for each of them seems to grow, not diminish. Your mother's and step-father's love will not be divided or shared between you and your new baby brother or sister - it will be increased. True, when the baby comes home, there will be more attention given to him or her, but that's a normal thing. When you were first born, your mother devoted much more of her time to you than she did to herself. However, this will be a time for you to bond with your new sibling. Your mother will need your help, and this will serve to bring the two of you closer together and in a new way. There's nothing wrong with the worries you are feeling. And there's nothing wrong with talking to your mother about your concerns. You are still, and always will be, her baby. Any mother worth the title will reassure you of that. Good luck. And congratulations on your new baby brother or sister. 
1|0@relationships^1438960069#ctuudoq:esmejones$:You should be more concerned that you'll be used as free babysitting all the time. Make sure you talk to your mom about her expectations for your help with the new sibling.
1|0@relationships^1438958173#ctut68p:cubenori$:So I have a brother who is 14 years younger than me. The mindset I got into was that I'm not going to fight for attention with the baby, but be on the other side giving the baby attention. Sort of a, if you can't beat them, join them, I suppose. 
1|0@relationships^1438958402#ctutben:AmbystomaMexicanum$:I can relate to what you're going through. I'm 21 and I have a 5 month old baby brother. When my dad and stepmom were going through IVF, I really hoped it wouldn't work. I was so against the idea of my 50 year old father having another child. I, too, feared that I would be replaced by the new, exciting child. If anything, having another baby made my father even more sentimental towards my other brother and I. He has become more attentive, loving, and kind towards us. Having a child turned him into a total sap. Above all, I love my baby brother so much it hurts. I can't imagine life without him. All I want to do is sit and hold him and stare at him. Sometimes I feel like he's my own child. There is something really special about being a much older sibling. I have a perspective that I didn't have when I was 5 years old and my first brother was born. It's one of the best things that's ever happened to me. Talk to your mom about your fears and worries. Try to involve yourself in her pregnancy instead of shutting her out or isolating yourself from the situation. It was hard for me to be excited at first, but the farther along the pregnancy progressed, the more overjoyed I was to be a big sister. Enjoy the ride! I hope this has helped reassure you. PM me if you have any questions or need someone to talk to. 
2|0@relationships^1439002196#ctvk00e:solonosolo$:Thanks for posting. I might PM you if I have more questions. 
1|0@relationships^1438958843#ctutl91:drzoidburger$:My friend's mom also got pregnant again when my friend was 15. My friend had a lot of the same feelings you described, and she was determined to be resentful of the new baby. 8 years later, she and her sister are super close, and she loves her so much. Because of the age difference, her sister views her more like a third parent than a traditional sister, and my friend is super protective of her. I think she's grown a lot from having a little sister, and her parents have also relaxed from tiger parents to more normal parents. 
3|0@relationships^1438924468#ctui1ky:Nickle_knuckles$:FWIW I'm the mom of an amazing wonderful brilliant little boy, and it's all of those things that make me want another child. I also really look forward to the relationship my son will have with his younger sibling. I bet your mom is also looking forward to your relationship with your baby sibling. I promise, though her focus will change, and you might feel less of a direct presence, she will not feel any differently about you. Ok, maybe she'll feel a little more pride when you take a part in your sibling's life, or a lot. She's going to be focused on the baby and then toddler, and really all that means is probably a more lenient curfew and some midnight diaper runs. Your feelings are completely valid, and I'm sure if you calmly told your mom how you feel "I feel silly even saying this, but lately I've been having some worries about X can we spend some one on one time together this weekend?" Bond with her over the new baby, show an interest and be prepared to help out with babycare. I'm sure your mom will be receptive if you talk to her, and try not to take any 'baby brain' too personally. You sound like a sweet kid. 
3|0@relationships^1438926206#ctuiq72:solonosolo$:Thank you for providing this perspective. It's helpful to understand why she might want another child. I actually would like to help out as much as I can. I think a big thing for me is just staying involved in the family and staying a part of it. I feel much better after all of the awesome responses. I feel prepared to talk to my mom and Pete so we could resolve this situation. 
3|0@relationships^1438926721#ctuix22:Nickle_knuckles$:That's awesome! You have SO much to look forward to as an older brother. You show real maturity in this post, especially your concern for stressing your mom out. You'll make a really great sibling, and I suspect a successful human in general. Good luck kid. 
5|0@relationships^1438924683#ctui4nd:LyssaBrisby$:You are so lovely. I am glad you're reading the good responses to this thread - I hate to think you're carrying this weight and this stress. Please, do bring your concerns to your mom! My eldest boy is just turning ten, and though he's much younger than you, I don't think there's much difference on this front. I recently had his baby brother and let me tell you, never once - NEVER ONCE - did the baby alter my love for him or his sister, except to strengthen it. I'm sure it may not even occur to her that you might feel insecure... because to her, there's nothing that could change her love for you. When I told my elder kids about the pregnancy, this is how I did it. I gave my eldest a cookie and said: when you were born, you had allll the love, alllllll the attention, everything was focused on you. Then your sister was born, and what happened? ...Being a sweetheart, he immediately went to break the cookie in half and share with his sister, but I stopped him. Not quite, I said - and gave her a cookie of her own. His sister didn't *divide* the love, she added even more to the family. Then I placed the third cookie by the empty chair -- and I said, "we're adding more love to our family." That's what a baby is, aside from an enormous messy hassle - it's the family growing, in size and in love. It speaks well of your character that you would like to contribute and help and even though it's not your job, anything you offer will be enormously valuable to your parents, and great life skills for you to pick up.
5|0@relationships^1438926512#ctuiucj:solonosolo$:Thank you. I have to say the your comment really hit home in a good way. 
3|0@relationships^1438944917#ctunqug:VaneFreja$:The cookie part made me cry fuzzy tears... I will definitely be using that some day! :)
9|0@relationships^1438924816#ctui6ma:lollipop4556$:Hey, I had the exact same situation as you, except I was a total brat about it. My mom was pregnant with my half-brother and only other sibling (she had it with my step-dad) when I was 15. I was SO hurt and upset. They wanted a baby so badly and I thought they just wanted to throw me away and start a new family together (and then I'd just be that awkward stepkid). I was so rude to everyone, showed NO interest in anything related to baby shopping, becoming a sibling, etc. and would frequently just cry and lock myself up in my room (looking back maybe I should have seen a school counselor). I was just so scared that my mom (who's my best friend and we're super close) wouldn't love me as much no matter how much she assured me she'd love me just the same. I was also scared my step-dad (who treated me like a true daughter) would discard me for his "real" kid. I literally felt like my days were numbered and my life was ending. It was a terrible 9 months honestly. The day the baby was born I cried and cried. All the pictures of me holding my brother for the first time show me looking so damn indifferent, it still hurts my mom to see them. I cried at least 10 times in the few weeks after he was born...I just didn't want to be thrown away. But do you know what else changed in my house, besides the fact that the baby was taking up time and energy? Nothing. I should've realized it from the start, but my parents love me just the same. And the baby I thought was ruining my entire life? I FUCKING LOVE THAT BABY. MY BROTHER IS MY EVERYTHING. My brother thinks I am literally the coolest person on the PLANET and even my parents know nothing can come between us. He's 6 now, and I'm literally superwoman to him. He comes running to me when my parents try to discipline him even though I'm a strict big sister too. Hell, once my mom took my phone as a punishment and he stole it from her room to return it to me! (My mom thought that was so funny she let me keep my phone) My entire family praises me for being a good sibling. My parents thank me for babysitting. My brother adores me. In the few months after he was born, I went from scowling when someone asked me to take care of him to missing him when I was in school! It won't be all rainbows though. It took time, there was some crying and yelling on my part even after I started to love him. I still sometimes felt like my (step)dad loved him more, but you have to realize this is the first baby he's raising. As you grow older his feelings should even out (if they don't please talk to him and your mom about this). Sorry for the long text, but I made this account just for this because reading this post made me feel like I was looking in a mirror. You will not be replaced. The baby will not magically be better than you. YOUR PARENTS WILL LOVE YOU THE SAME! AND YOU WILL LOVE THE BABY AND THE BABY WILL LOVE YOU! All siblings fight (me and my bro still do lol) and having such a big age gap means that your sibling-relationship will be different than other people's (but no less valuable). But PLEASE discuss how you're feeling with your mom and let her reassure you herself. And try to enjoy this pre-baby time and don't be a downer :)
2|0@relationships^1438927250#ctuj3ww:solonosolo$:That you for responding. I'm glad everything worked out for you. I hope the same happens for me. 
3|0@relationships^1438929245#ctujrxa:maidrin$:It will. You're a young man but you haven't really encountered loving a baby yet. The thing is, once you love and raise a little baby into a young person, they got you forever. Unless your mom is a psychopath (and she's obviously not from how you talk about her) she's gonna only love you more every day. Now, you're gonna end up having a little baby to love too. It's gonna annoy the hell out of you some, but just wait till it says words and hugs you and depends on you. You're not losing a mom, you're gaining a sibling at maybe the most awesome time you could... I'm actually jealous! Talk to mom. She's gonna understand. Your feelings are normal and won't stress her. Just do it early when you're both calm, don't wait till you're stressed and the anxiety is out of control. 
4|0@relationships^1438925475#ctuig1c:bears2013$:You're getting a sbling--that's a lifelong thing. It kind of sucks getting a sibling when you're already older instead of having one near your age, but only in that the relationship will be different, not that the bond won't be as strong. I love my little sister to death--she's a lot younger than me, but I'm almost like a third parent to her. I can't imagine my life without her. Not to be morbid, but when my parents won't be around anymore, she'll be the closest family I'll have left. Yes, the baby will take up a lot more time and attention. But don't misinterpret that as them loving you any less--it's just that you're an almost self-sufficient teenager, and your new sibling will be a helpless tiny thing that literally can't survive without constant attention and care. They'll ooh and aahh at its first words and scramble to record its first steps--but your mom did that with you as a baby, and now the new baby will get the same love and attention you got from her when you were young. 
