5|0@relationships^1438928417#ctuji49:ThrownMaxibon$:Small children are not too hard to handle. Your mum will be doing most of the care, like diapers and feeding, but all kids from the age of 4 to 12 want attention. It's why lots of kids act out, even for bad attention. If you sit down with the little booger eater in 8 years time and just let them show you their cool bottle cap collection they will think you're the coolest guy ever. You reading to the kid will probably be one of its favourite childhood memories. 
3|0@relationships^1438926651#ctuiw6r:HowlingBukowski$:This happened to me twice while I was still living with my mother and step father, the first time I was about your age and I was furious, not because of the potential sibling but because my mother lied about it when I directly asked if she was pregnant. It caused an even bigger rift between us as a family. She had a miscarriage and as bad as this sounds I'm kind of glad, I was a shitty teenager with a pile of mental health issues I was trying to work through with an alcoholic abusive ptsd riddled stepdad trying to get sober. When she got pregnant again she was 42 I was 18 and was in my first year of university (still living at home). This time I wasn't angry but I'll admit I was a little sad, during my stepdads previous arguments with my mother over the years he'd said that I didn't count as his child because I wasn't blood even though he adopted me at age 10. His father said it too and I was just sad for about it for a long time and I probably drank too much at parties or was a little too adventurous with drugs because of it. My mother tried to make an effort to include me but it didn't really happen and you know that was ok, after a while I warmed up to the idea of having a sibling. Started buying baby clothes and going baby shopping with my mom. It was ok for a while, until they (I'm guessing she was pushed by stepfather a little) asked me to move out in May after my semester ended, my lease on my first apartment didn't start Til the end of July and my brother wasn't due to be born until October 1 so of course I was crushed. In my mind my mother was making me homeless so they could redo my room into my new brothers room. I managed to find a place to stay during those two months but I did something's I'm not proud of in exchange for a living space. When my brother was born no one called or even texted to tell me, I found out via pictures on facebook, and I cried like a bitch. I later (over a year later) learnt than my grandmother (stepdads side) had asked when I was getting there and had I visited yet that my mother lied and said I was coming later...I never got a call or text to come visit...I didn't get to see my brother for 3 weeks. Now my brother is 2 and a half and he's the funniest little boy with the dirtiest old man laugh you could ever imagine. He calls me sissy and tells me he loves me even though I only see him maybe once every 3 months. He knows who I am because he's not a replacement for me, he's just a new part of my family; just like your new sibling will be to you. My point in this awful rambling is that yes it's weird and you don't quite know what to do but trust me when I say your mom can only grow to love you MORE from this experience not less. She'll look over one day to see you and your sibling having a moment together and her heart will swell with pride. It's a great thing that you want to be involved and help her, start small if you don't know what to do to help her, do more chores without her asking, learn how to make simple dinners for when she's too tired to cook and maybe read up a little on newborns just so you know what's going on. Also get excited with her, you two can still do things together while she's pregnant, go out to lunch and stroll through babies r us start talking about your sibling with her, name ideas, books you want to read them; start doing things for your future sibling :) Things may get rocky because babies are hard and hormones suck but there is no way your mom will stop loving you, you're her first born, she knows that this sibling will always have your protection and she won't have to worry. Your mom sounds like a good mom and you seem to be an excellent and willing son, it's hard not being the baby anymore but it's great being the person your younger sibling looks up to. 
2|0@relationships^1438927812#ctujaun:solonosolo$:I'm sorry how things turned out. I hope you have a better relationship with your parents now. My mom is a good mom. I'm very lucky. 
