6|0@relationships^1438927277#ctuj4a5:Floomby$:Your mom and Pete will be extremely involved with the baby when s/he is born, to the exclusion of almost anything else, for the first few months. The good news is that after the first few weeks, every time you offer to help with the baby, they will be thrilled. There is no freaking way the baby will hate you unless s/he is Dr. Evil in infant form. If you play your cards right, the baby will adore you, even more so when you go to college and come back like a bad ass conquering hero. When the baby is a toddler, s/he will break one or more expensive and valuable things of yours. That is just the order of Nature. You did it too. I'm guessing this was not a planned pregnancy. I may be wrong; that's just my guess. There is no question that the baby will take up significant family resources, because that's what children do. Whether or not this is a problem depends how on the edge your family is. 
1|0@relationships^1438928553#ctujjpv:maidrin$:This is a tough age to get another (and your first) sibling. Your emotions are normal for a child, and you're on the cusp of adulthood. That combination is fine, but it might be adding to your anxiety a bit. You say your mom has always loved you so much, and that you're happy for her and don't want to hurt or worry her. I think those two things mean that everything is going to be fine if you are willing to use some mature communication skills (which you are totally going to do a good job at, based on your post!). I was an only child and I think that creates a certain bond between parent and child, which I think is maybe what you're afraid of losing. But you're not going to lose that bond at all, I promise, because she's been with you from infanthood to becoming a young man already. Why do I think that? Because she already has a quasi-adult relationship with you. I've seen it with many of my friends who are considerably older than their younger siblings, and even with my husband (who is ten years older than his closest sibling). You're family and you're a young man she's raised and can depend upon, right? You're not losing your mom at all. You're gaining a little person to get to know and love in a whole new way. Yes, there will be things that irritate you, like baby showers and how old aunts ignore you for the new baby (temporarily). But your mom will love, LOVE seeing you step into a new role as big brother. And yanno, you're probably going to love it too. Now back to communication. If you feel too anxious to talk to mom in person, write her a heartfelt letter just like what you've told us. Your written communication is great and I'm sure she'll understand. Have a talk/write the letter -before- baby arrives so she's on the same page. Things will be ok. Keep in mind that only children are relatively rare in human history, mums totally have enough love to go around, AND you're in the wonderful, unique position of being practically grown up so you can support and connect with your mum in a way that no new baby can. Your mom sounds lovely and she's raised a great young man. Communication can help you feel more secure. 
5|0@relationships^1438928851#ctujnak:GotHighAndWroteThis$:-Your mother will never love you less. No matter what you ever do or how awesome your sibling is. -This kid will be easier because she already did this all once. She will probably let him or her get away with a lot more than you did. It happens regardless of age gap. -Yes, you will take the back burner for a bit. Newborns and toddlers are HARD and time consuming. Try to understand this and be a help to your mom during this time. Also understand her hormones will be crazy and she will be extremely sleep deprived. It's nothing personal if she gets snippy with you a few times. 
1|0@relationships^1438929945#ctuk01n:solonosolo$:I will try to keep that in mind and be more patient. 
1|0@relationships^1438931580#ctukhx4:GotHighAndWroteThis$:You will do awesome. Kids are hard, but fun. You have an awesome opportunity because you get to be more of a cool uncle. Your relationship with your parents change as you get older too, so try not to attribute any of that to your sibling. 
2|0@relationships^1438929025#ctujpdq:JesusListensToSlayer$:My story isn't identical, but here you go: I was 12 when my baby brother was born - yes, quite a bit younger than you, he was the last of 5, and also I'm a girl. But I *loved* this kid. He was just so comforting to me - especially around ages 6 months to 2 years (which coincided with some rough adolescent times for me.) I didn't have the best relationship with my mom at the time, and when we'd get in a fight, I'd grab the baby to cheer myself up. I'd go sit in the comfy chair and just hold him. I always felt better. It's like he was my favorite stuffed animal. The little shit's 25 now, and he's getting married next month. I'll probably fall apart at the wedding and embarrass the crap out of him. My point, though, is that there is so much potential joy in this situation. I know it's hard to see the future from your perspective, but just know that it could actually enrich your life in a way that most 16 year-olds will never know. Don't get too attached though...they grow up so fast! Sniff. 
