1|0@relationships^1438951956#ctuq09l:chocolatephantom$:Hi there, as a mother of 2 children I can tell you that you don't take part of your love for the 1st to love the 2nd. No, in fact your love just grows. I love my daughter. She is independent, funny and just like me in so many ways. I feel like I know her inside out. AND I love my son. He is sporty and active and loves to cuddle. He's such a handsome little boy. You and your mum have had 16 wonderful years to build up an amazing bond. This baby could never replace you in your mums heart, I guarantee it. Just think about it, you are going to have the benefits of a baby without all the hard work. It will be amazing. I hope everything goes well and if you're feeling unsure talk to your mum. She loves you!
1|0@relationships^1438952040#ctuq1i5:hellomarvelousthings$:Warning, this is a super lengthy response!: When I was 12 my mom sat my brother me down for a "family meeting." We've never had anything close to a family meeting so I assumed something cool was about to happen like getting another dog...Nope! No new dog. She was having a baby... I immediately started bawling. I must have cried for days. I didn't want anything to change about the family dynamic we already had and I was scared everything would be about the baby now. A few days later, as I was finally getting used to the idea of a new baby, we had another family meeting: SHE WAS HAVING TWINS. My crying continued. Anyway, I understand being upset at the idea of a new baby. It's normal to think that your mom might like the other baby more but this won't be the case. What might help to calm you down is to keep in mind that there's a huge age difference between you and the baby! Once the baby is born, you're capable of helping your mom take care of him/her. Babies are stressful. She WILL need your help and she's going to love you more than ever for it. Even something as small as offering to sit with the baby for 20 minutes so that she has time to take a shower is incredibly helpful. There's only a 12 year difference between my sisters and me, but I've always felt kind of like a mom to them. When they were born, I still wasn't excited about them. But I knew that my mom, while happy about having babies, was stressed out and tired. I helped her whenever I could. Fed them, babysat them, just about anything except changing diapers (my middle school self could not handle dirty diapers). It wasn't always easy and it wasn't always fun, I can't say I didn't complain from time to time, but my mom always made it clear that she appreciated my help. I felt that this was when my mom really started treating me like more of an adult than a child and we became much closer than we already were. I'm 21 now and my sisters are 9. I don't live with my family anymore, but I can easily say that my sisters are the coolest little people in my life! They're so sweet and they say the most hilarious things. I still help to take care of them and hang out with them when I can. I'll take them out for froyo, help them with homework, volunteer for their Girl Scout troop. I hope we'll remain as close when we get older. I actually recently met my boyfriend's much older cousins (like in their 50s/60s). They are sisters that are 12 years apart, they're best friends, they live together AND they work together. Meeting them made me so happy because I hope that my sisters and I can be as close when we're that age (although I can't see myself wanting to live with them ha). And my mom and I have only gotten closer over the past 9 years! Tl;dr You're gonna be fine! 
1|0@relationships^1438952453#ctuq7jm:dripless_cactus$:I truly believe that love is not a zero sum game. Meaning, there isn't a finite pool of love that must be shared between the people you have relationships with in your life. Love grows, and when there are more people in your life to love, you will find you have increased capacity. This is true for friends, children, cousins... Your mom isn't replacing you with a baby. A baby and a 16 year old are completely different experiences. You'll always be her first born. She might have less *time* for others besides the baby, especially in that first year, but that is out of necessity, not necessary because she loves the baby more. Worries and fears about being left behind and being excluded are normal. But try not to get hung up on the negative "what-ifs". What if your new sibling is awesome? What if he looks up to you and adores you? What if you do indeed become good friends and co-conspirators? These possibilities are at least equally as likely. Plus it's not like you don't have some influence over the way this goes. Good luck. I hope you start to feel better. Babies mean big changes which can be scary, but change doesn't have to be bad.
