1|0@relationships^1438970613#ctv1hna:ElbowIsAWenis$:You're 16. Use the baby as an opportunity to do *whatever the fuck you want* without recourse. It'll be great. 
1|0@relationships^1438970812#ctv1mpj:ElleCay$:I have been through this exactly. One month before I turned 17, my dad's girlfriend gave birth to my first brother. I graduated high school and went off to university the next year, so I didn't live with my brother and we did not grow up together. School was close, though, so I came home and helped out. A lot. I was their go-to babysitter, and I volunteered to babysit often. I changed diapers, taught him letters and numbers, and attended every school event, sports game, and birthday party. I was more like an aunt than a sister. We have a different relationship than my brother does with his parents, but that's because I'm an independent adult and he's still a kid. I got my dad to myself for almost my entire childhood, whereas my brother always had to share him with me. My relationship with my dad is really good because he knows he can count on me. And I can still count on him. I've had a few crises in the past few years and my dad dropped everything to come help me. I'm still his first born, his baby girl, 30 years old. And my brother, who is now 13, and I have a really good relationship, too. Help out where you can. Involve yourself in the baby's life from day 1. Your relationship with your mom will change, but it was going to anyway as you grow up. Make plans for your future and take the necessary steps to get there. Be a good role model for your little brother. Your mom stepdad will appreciate you, which will keep you in a special place in their heart that your little brother will not have, no matter how much they love him. 
1|0@relationships^1438973650#ctv3lx9:cathline$:You have a whole life opening in front of you! High school, then college, and getting your first job and going to homecoming and the prom, and practicing your sport and growing your interests ---- You really don't need to be spending a lot of time around the house at your age. Especially since you will probably be enlisted as a babysitter fairly quickly. You WANT to have a sport lined up for practice. You WANT to have a club you are an active participant in. It's far more likely that you will be pressed into babysitter service while mom goes out with her bf. You will get to know more about poopy diapers and cleaning spit-up than you are probably ready for. 
0|0@relationships^1438979804#ctv7pxo:stampadhesive$:This is probably a surprise for your mom, too. She probably thought she was starting menopause and boom, she's pregnant. I have lots of friends this has happened to. Trust me, that kid is going to adore you, look up to you, and want to hang out all the time. Your going off to college is going to be sooooo hard on the kid. Make sure you call constantly or they'll feel some abandonment. Your mom won't love you any less. Just be prepared to help out around the house more. If you can drive, expect to run a lot of errands because going on errands with an infant is difficult. Also, be prepared to babysit for free. But invite the girls in your class over to see the baby. They'll love it. 
1|0@relationships^1439001751#ctvjs1m:solonosolo$:Hopefully I 'll be close enough that I can come back some weekends, but I'll definitely call her text them goodnight as much as possible. 
1|0@relationships^1438979127#ctv79ri:modestmint$:My dad had a baby when I was 17, and I felt really similar to the way you're feeling. I was very distressed. I burst into tears when he told me he and his girlfriend were expecting and my reaction came as a total shock to him. He thought I would be overjoyed. Your mom probably has no idea you're feeling this way, too. Go out for lunch together, just the two of you, and lay out how you're feeling on the table. Express your fears and feelings of insecurity. My sister is 3 now and I love her very much. Ours is not so much a sibling bond, but probably more comparable to an aunt/niece relationship. You might find yourself feeling better once your new sibling shows they are happy to see you when you walk in the door. (:
