3|0@relationships^1438939098#ctumcba:ntrophi$:I was 16 when my baby brother was born, and my mum was the same age as yours; I was not in exactly the same situation as you (I have two other younger brothers, so I wasn't an only child) but I remember having some similar fears. I was more involved in my mum's pregnancy (to the point of holding her hand when she went into labour, though I wasn't there for the birth itself) and honestly it's pretty super gross. You can be helpful; help her out, let her know that you're there for her and when it gets towards the end of the pregnancy, she's going to struggle with a lot of stuff and feel a bit rubbish and just knowing that you're there to support her will be a massive help to her. I'm guessing that you guys are pretty close? You should definitely tell her what you're feeling; hell, she might even have similar fears about how you're taking it. Holding it all in and trying to deal with it yourself is probably the worst thing you can do. As for the baby, in the beginning it will be tough. Babies are loud and gross and demanding, but you have to remember that they don't do it on purpose. Demanding all the time from mum is all they know. But they can also be adorable. I remember sitting and watching TV with my baby brother asleep on my chest and just feeling really really happy that he was here. And it gets better as they get older; I used to hang out with him a lot before he was 5, and he used to idolise me. Even now (he's 16 now), he has a lot in common with me and we can talk really easily about all sorts of things. Out of all of my siblings, I probably have the best relationship with him now. I love that he feels he can come to me with stuff that he doesn't want to talk to my parents about, but that he wants some kind of "grown up" advice about, but we can also talk about music and games and everything else inbetween. We talk about his plans for university and what he wants to do when he leaves school and stuff and it's just awesome. Yes, my mum still spoils him because he's her baby and he always will be, but it wasn't as big a deal as I thought it would be. Yes, she had to spend a lot more time with him when he was young, but that wasn't because she loved me any less - he needed her time more than I did. I was able to feed myself and wash myself and all that stuff that he couldn't. So yeah, it'll be rough in the beginning, but all babies are rough. It'll be a big change for you and your mum and Pete, but change is not always a bad thing. Tell your mum how you feel, and I bet she'll do all she can to assuage your fears. And as for the baby, it's going to think that you're the best big brother! It will all be awesome :) 
3|0@relationships^1438940975#ctums1m:ilovedogssfm$:One of my high school friends had a brother when she was 16. She was in your same situation. Her mom had just married her boyfriend and she worried about all the things you are worrying about rn! And you know what? She loves her brother ! She once told me she kinda feels like she helped raising him so its very special for her. And even tho they dont share a lot in common because of the age gap, her brother loves her a lot and looks up to her which is cool. And her moms boyfriend still loves my friend as his own child. And her mom loves her even more because my friend now kinda understands what her mom went through when she (my friend) was little which only makes her appreciate her mom even more. Also there's a new complicity with her mom since she's older and understands things her brother doesn't yet Everything's gonna be alright OP! Just voice your concerns to your mom. She will be glad you told her and you can talk about it. 
