6|0@relationships^1438920566#ctugbo7:magikarpcandosplash$:Let me tell you that this baby is going to be great. I'm the eldest of four and my youngest brother is just three years old. When he was born I was 18. I don't have the same fears as you because I'm the eldest anyway (and my parents are pretty shitty), but believe me when I say that this baby will be the most wonderful gift you've got. No, I can't promise you that you'll still be the priority in the family because you definitely won't be. Your parents would pay less and less attention to you, especially because there's a baby there who would need their attention more, but believe me when I say that it won't matter anyway--because you'll pay attention to the baby more too. I practically raised my youngest brother. I rocked him to sleep, fed him, calmed him down, wiped his shit stained bum, everything. It was to the point where he was more attached to me than our mom during his infancy. It changed after I went to a summer school for a month when he was six months old because I wasn't his primary caregiver anymore, but damn do I love him like I imagine I would my own kid. OP, be involved. You'll realise that the love of a kid is amazing. I cried when he was born and sometimes when I rocked him too, because you have this small living human and you know they love you blindly. The baby's such a small living being who rely completely on you, and it's a beautiful thing. 
15|0@relationships^1438921149#ctugllk:PosterGirlWNoPoster$:You've gotten a lot of great advice here, but one thing I want to add is this: babies absolutely take a lot of work, but for the first year or so, it's physical work, not so much mental work. You're constantly feeding them, changing them, making sure they don't accidentally kill themselves, etc... but you're not really intellectually engaging with them. Which means that if you don't mind hanging out with your mom while she sits on the couch breastfeeding for like 22 hours a day, you can actually get plenty of time with her, since the kind of attention you'll be wanting is very different (and typically not in conflict with) the kind that the baby will need. And that will last for probably a good year and a half- my youngest is almost two, and for a long time I don't really think his older brother minded the loss of physical attention so much because he still got 100% of the conversation. Also, pregnant ladies are notorious for wanting to talk about their pregnancies far more than anyone else wants to hear about their pregnancies- it's entirely possible that your mom assumes that the LAST thing a 16 year old boy wants to hear about is what fruit the fetus currently resembles or the relative merits of infant vs. convertible carseats, and has been trying to keep it to a minimum around you. If you actually want to be more involved, definitely let her know that- I'm sure she'd be THRILLED to have free rein to talk to you more about what's happening with the pregnancy and plans for the new baby. There are lot of reasons to have more kids, and I don't at all think it would be unreasonable to ask your mom and Pete to verbalize those with you. I know that for my husband and me, a big part of wanting multiple kids was really valuing our own relationships with our siblings and wanting our kids to have that- I bet if you ask, your mom will tell you that one of the things she's most excited about in relation to the new baby is seeing you and your little sibling together and the relationship that you'll develop. I love my kids on their own, but I swear I probably love them most when they're being awesome to each other. And to be honest, while I'm a little younger than your mom, I'm at a point where a lot of my friends are starting to deal with elder care and other family issues, and that stuff makes me SO grateful that I have a sibling, and that my kids will at least have each other. When I had a second kid, I don't feel like I got my older son a replacement, I feel like I got him a teammate. (He would probably say that I got him a sidekick)
