4|0@relationships^1438955608#cturpgr:nick_locarno$:Dude, I had a second kid because the first one was so awesome. Love isn't finite. The more you love, the more you want to love more.
49|0@relationships^1438921736#ctugvgo:BigBadMrBitches$:I'm the baby of the family. My brother was 12 and my sister was 16. I didn't replace anyone except my brother at the sink on dish days.
2|0@relationships^1438935573#ctull24:yung_leen$:Hey, it'll be okay! The same thing happened to me a year ago! There's 18 years difference between us. It's a big change, especially when it's just been you two. It was the same for me, and me and my mum were really close. You're not going to be the only child anymore so you probably won't get as much attention, but your mum (and her SO) will get to see you as a brother! So they'll not only love you as much as they already do for being who you are now, they will love you even more seeing you bond with this tiny little person. The best part by far though, is seeing your brother/sister grow and develop, and I am willing to bet you any money they will adore you. I have never felt happier than have my brother waddle up to me as fast as he could smiling and hugging me. They will love you so much it's ridiculous. Your relationship with your mum will probably change, but provided you don't hate your sibling, the experience can really bring you two much closer. She'll appreciate things a lot more - it'll be harder now she's older and might not have the energy to do everything she did with you when you were a baby with them. If you decide to help out, great! That would probably help even more. I remember reading on here something about someone saying that we are taught from very early on that everything is finite - money, time, you get the gist. But love isn't finite - love can grow and grow and grow. Speak to your mum, because I would bet she also has fears about being pregnant again - and what impact that might have on your relationship. Just try to think about the positives, communicate with the people you love (and that love you) and you will be absolutely fine :) 
2|0@relationships^1438935904#ctulo0e:solitarygrimangel$:Love is not a finite resource. You are not replaceable.
1|0@relationships^1438936561#ctultvt:Spiritpony$:The age difference between my oldest sibling and my youngest sibling is 15 years, not too different from your situation. They get along great. My older brother is a kind of an uncle type of figure to us in a way. My siblings and I haven't felt like one of us stole our dad. Talk to your mom about your feelings, you'll be fine, OP. 
3|0@relationships^1438930791#ctuk9ak:that-frakkin-toaster$:I see tons of awesome advice in here but thought I'd share my own experiences as well. My sister is 10 years older than me. So not quite 16 but still a lot older. We didn't get along at all when I was little, but somewhere around my teen years we grew very close. We live in different states now but we visit each other often, and talk almost every day. Don't stress your relationship with your sibling though. It will happen or it won't, but it will change so many times over time. and really, by the time that kid is old enough to like or dislike you, you'll be an adult, have a couple years of dealing with the baby under your belt already, and you'll be mentally prepared to be an awesome big brother. Your relationship will be largely dictated by you. I used my sister as an example... A lot of why we didn't get along was because she was mean when I was little. She didn't want me around. When I was older and we had things in common that changed. But she was the type to party and drink and do drugs, not the type who wanted to come over and play dolls or whatever little me wanted to do back then. If you want to teach your little sibling how to fish or play a game on your Xbox 250000 when they're older, you can. I am a mother to one child right now. I want to tell you why I want to have more. Because my child is awesome! She is still young but she is silly and smart and sassy, and she can be a huge pain in the ass, but she is my child and there is no love that compares. And to have another kid, and have all those experiences again, will be amazing. Someone else said, love is not finite. And that is something I never really understood until I had a kid of my own. I'm sure that sounds ridiculous. Love for your kids is just... different. I wish I had the words to explain it to you. As far as "favorites" go, well, yes and no. I have a favorite parent, sometimes. I love my parents completely and equally. But some days my mother is the one I am closer to and other days it is my father. I am sure it is the same way with kids, because I see that my mom is like that with me and my sister. Your mom will love you the same no matter what. One day you might be the favorite because she values having a more adult conversation with you. The next day you might piss her off(as teens often do), and the baby will be the favorite. But none of that means she loves you more or less. I also wanted to put a note in about Pete. My older sister is my half sister. But she calls my dad her dad. They love each other as if she was his. Having his own blood daughter changed none of that. In fact, sometimes I am even a tad jealous of their bond. They've had much more time to cultivate their relationship than I have with my father. Now, I don't know Pete, but if he has always treated you well, I highly doubt adding another kid in there is going to change it. I know words don't help as much as they should. It is almost too bad you have to sit and wait for the baby to come, because you have nothing to do but think! But having a baby won't change the fundamental core of who your mom and Pete are, or how they feel about you. It will likely change their demeanor and morale for a while... It is more exhausting than I thought possible. I mean in high school I was in like 3 extra curriculars including band (which took 2 weeknights and all day Saturday), and worked 30+ hours a week. I slept like 4-5 hours a day. And I was tired. But man I did not know tired until I had a baby. So they'll probably be short tempered and seem busy. But they will also be so relieved that you are 16 and capable of taking care of yourself! I have a feeling that more than anything, having this baby will make your mom MORE proud of you. I hope you talk to your mom. I hope you find some reassurance from her and the comments here. If you still feel these insecurities and stress, it might be worth mentioning to your mother that you are interested in some counseling. I know those are dreaded words but a therapist might have the right tools to prepare you for such a huge change. Please believe us when we say your mom will not forget you. As a mom, my kid is my pride and joy. And completely irreplaceable. 
2|0@relationships^1438931151#ctukd8z:solonosolo$:I'm glad so many moms responded to my post. It's been nice hearing your own stories and how you feel about your children. It's very reassuring and comforting to know that you love your kids equally. 
1|0@relationships^1438931032#ctukbyw:rikk0s$:It's very normal for children to feel replaced by or jealous of new siblings. When I was 17 my mother told me she became pregnant. I'm not going to say the first few years were easy, being a loud, rambunctious teenager is pretty much impossible with a baby in the house. But I will tell you what, I have an eight year old sister now who means the world to me. 
1|0@relationships^1438931766#ctukjy2:whatglasses5$:I'm definitely not judging you! I can say from experience that this baby will be an addition, and not a replacement. This baby is going to look up to you. I always wanted a big brother but instead I have significantly older sisters. I am really grateful for them. They went through everything first and were able to give me advice. If you work on cultivating a good relationship, it can be so much fun. Siblings are awesome! Your feelings are not invalid and I can totally understand why you feel that way, but it really will be okay. Talking to your mom about this will probably help a lot. Give her the chance to reassure you - honestly, it sounds like that would do a lot of good. 
