3|0@relationships^1438943508#ctundx5:Lydious$:I'm a much older sister to my baby sister. I'm 34, she's 12, and its AMAZING. We get along great, and we have a great relationship with no jealousy partially because we didn't grow up together fighting over toys and attention. She adores me and won't leave me alone whenever I come to visit. I'm more fun than mom, but I'm still an adult so I can take her places and look out for her. Large age gaps between siblings are really a blessing in disguise. Of course your mom will love you just as much. That's a totally normal fear for someone your age to have, but parents dont have a baby and then dismiss the older child cause they aren't needed anymore. If anything, you'll be needed MORE to help with feedings &amp; diaper changes when mom &amp; dad haven't slept in days. I practically raised my sister so my mom could go back to work and it was really great cause we got to build a strong bond. There is nothing so rewarding as caring for and guarding a helpless little creature, and having them cling to you and smile at you while you hold them. Your new sibling isn't a replacement for you, its an addition to the love and joy you guys already have. There will be times when its frustrating and stressful, but overall it'll be a lot of fun. You'll probably get a bit less attention, but only because a baby requires so much more attention than a teenager. You'll get to help take care of him or her, and help guide this new little life on their path to becoming a person. You get to be the cool big brother that this kid is going to idolize and look up to. You'll gain valuable experience that will help you be a better father when/if you have kids of your own someday. You guys are gonna have so much fun together, I promise. I'm an old lady but when me &amp; my sister get together, were BOTH 12. We play pranks on our mom, tease each other, wrestle, fart on each other, etc. We have just as much fun as litter-mate siblings, but I'm old enought to be her mentor as well. Its the best of both worlds and I wouldn't give her up for anything. TL;DR: You're gonna be fine. Everything will be fine. Deep breath :)
1|0@relationships^1438944477#ctunmsv:grimrogue$:Though this is late to the party, as a person with brothers 16 and 14 years younger, you wont be replaced. In fact as the years go on, she may turn to you as a vent person. And it makes a great form of birth control. Having 2 screaming babies/toddlers made me much more condom wearing aware.
1|0@relationships^1438945185#ctunt9v:emmiemay$:I'm sure suddenly getting a younger sibling when you're 16 is really scary for you but if you're worried about competition for your mum you should remember how different a baby and a teenager are - with such a big age difference you two have completely different niches in your Mum's life. The baby can't have the kind of verging on adult relationship that you'll be starting to have with your Mum in the next few years. The upside of this is now hopefully for the rest of your life you'll have this entire extra person to love and to be an awesome big brother too - even if the change seems scary now. 
33|0@relationships^1438915932#ctudy5l:Buddhamama42$:Hello again ! So here I am in person. It looks like the trolls have already moved in on this post - just ignore - I've reported them. So here's the thing - you've been a single child all your life, so of course you're nervous about that situation changing. This is a completely normal and natural response. And a new baby is a BIG change :) Your Mum and Pete sound like they love you a lot. I think the best thing you can do is to ask to sit down with them and to just let them know that you're a bit nervous and unsure about this. Also ask what you can do to help. New babies are pretty much feeding, pooping machines - but there are things you can do to help your Mum - step up with the chores a bit. Can you cook ? Do laundry ? Because now would be a good time to learn if you can't. If you show that you are a bit nervous and unsure, but that you want to step up and help, then you are showing maturity on two levels - that firstly you can talk openly about your feelings without being whiny or demanding; and that secondly you want to contribute more as a household member to help out. Be aware that the first few months *will* be difficult. Your Mum and Pete may be very, very tired and if your Mum is breastfeeding she'll be paying a LOT of attention to the baby. Honestly, *anything* you can do at this time will help. Learn how to hold your baby sibling. Ask to be shown how to change its nappy. Learn how to cook a quick, delicious meal that your Mum can eat with one hand :) If you can just cuddle and hold your baby sibling while your Mum has a shower it will make a huge difference to her at this time. I'm NOT saying that you have to co-parent this baby !! But if you can help out, it will make this period a lot easier on all of you. Nevertheless, expect your Mum and Pete to be a bit more short tempered or grumpy due to sleep deprivation - it WILL pass. Is your Mum reading baby books ? Like "What To Expect When You're Expecting" or the Penelope Leach Baby book ? Because if she is, it wouldn't hurt for you to have a flick through and see what she's going through and what to expect.... You can also go onto /r/parenting - they're a helpful bunch there and ask what you can do to help make your Mum comfortable. I will emphasise again that its NOT your job, but that every little you can do will help, and you've said that you want to help make your Mum happy and comfortable. You sound like a thoughtful and intelligent young man - I'm sure that you can work something out with your Mum and Pete that will allow you to enjoy your new baby sibling's arrival.
13|0@relationships^1438916833#ctuefue:solonosolo$:Thank you for taking the time to write all of this. I'm going to try to involve myself as much as I can and keep in mind what you said. 
1|0@relationships^1438971608#ctv26xh:Usrname52$:You also can get involved *before* the baby. I think that it will make both u ou and your mom happier to know you are on board with this baby and want to be supportive. Ask to go shopping with your mom for baby clothes and stuff for the nursery and other things they might need to plan. Maybe see if you can be involved in the naming decision (unless they have a specific name for some reason). Offer to do some more chores even now, because your mom is going to be tired and stressed. This will give you the opportunity to feel more adult and for your mom to see you that way. She will still love you, and will develop more respect for you as a person. And dude, talking about being a great older brother to a toddler is totally going to get you laid (with college age girls) in college.
37|0@relationships^1438917243#ctuenam:restlessgrad$:Yes, the baby will get all the attention for a while. That's what babies do. They need to be fed and changed every 2 hours for the first few months. You will be forgotten for a while. You'll have to be much more independent. You know what? That was going to happen anyway. You're 2 years away from 18, when you'll be an adult. At your age, most kids are learning how to cook and meal plan for themselves, handle all of their own chores and laundry, and are learning to make and manage their own money. All the stuff you need to learn to be an adult. For you, this baby will probably help you do that - you'll have to be much more independent. And that's okay. Because you were on that track anyway. Love is not finite. There's no reason to be jealous of a baby. Your mom will love the baby as much as she loves you, and she'll dote on the baby much more. There's enough love to go around and it multiplies; she won't love you any less by loving this baby a ton. Parents might **like** one kid more than another at different points (they'll like you more when the baby is smearing the walls with its own shit, they'll like the baby more when it walks for the first time and you're screaming about how you didn't get the car on the weekend). But loving different people equally really is possible and it's the way most parents feel. Throughout your life, you're going to have the opportunity to react with jealousy and anger. Instead, focus on the positive. You're getting a little brother/sister! What an incredible gift! This person will always be there for your parents once you've moved out. You are no longer alone in managing and taking care of your parents as they age. You have a whole new person who you can show the entire world to - and you can watch them grow up. This baby is a huge gift. Talk to your mom - again, you'll always be faced with the choice between a) emotional honesty/intimacy b) keeping everything to yourself. It's okay to share. 
