11|0@relationships^1438943988#ctuni7y:silveredblue$:Hey! There's actually a 16 year age gap between me (20F) and my youngest brother (4M). I love him and his adorable ideas and plans for the world, and he loves and looks up to me so much that he cries his little heart out when I have to go back to college. :( You totally can have a great relationship with your new baby sibling! Plus, if you help with taking care of him or her you will learn SO much to prepare you to be a father yourself one day. S/he will say the funniest things as a toddler too. Recently my brother asked "what's this part called?" as he tapped his arm. I answered, "That's your forearm!" Astonished he looked down and said, "But then...where is my threearm??l
1|0@relationships^1438969670#ctv0tjw:phantomrhiannon$:I have three older siblings, and the nearest one in age to me is 8 years older (gap up to 12 years). There were some things that sucked on both ends. I had TOO MANY DAMN PARENTS, and they all had this annoying little kid in all their business. But I felt like I'd been awarded a gold medal when my brother finally deemed me responsible enough to borrow his books, and I had three older siblings to go to bat for me if they ever thought my parents were being too restrictive. Now that we're all adults, I have great relationships with them. Better than a few of them have with each other, because we never accrued any resentment over high school drama or conflicting parenting styles or whatever else you fight about with people the same age. It's not always going to be great, but with some patience and understanding, you'll find a new normal and have a new friend for life.
10|0@relationships^1438917351#ctuep9g:MissyBear2$:First of all, what you are feeling is actually really really really normal. They have all sorts of books out there (usually aimed for toddlers because most families are not spaced out so big) about how mommies don't love the new babies more. And yeah. There's going to be some displacement in terms of family time. Also you should know that babies cry loudly. A lot. At night. And just in general. It's going to be a real test of your patience. I suggest you talk with your mom, not only about having "special" time with you, but you having an established "escape plan." Somewhere you can go at a moment's notice to get some peace to either sleep or study. A friend's house or somewhere else. And littleorangemonkies' advice is really good as well. At 16 you're old enough to drive. Now would be an EXCELLENT time to practice real world skills like compiling grocery lists, staying on top of the trash and stuff like that. If you can practice cooking that's a bonus as well! Everything will be alright man. You'll be just fine. Just talk to your mom, and be honest. Write down key points if you need to before hand. You can do this. And you're going to grow so much because of it. Also, all kids ADORE their big brothers/sisters. You're older and therefore cool. When the kid gets old enough, don't blow it by making promises you can't keep. Also candy helps. But just spend time with the kid. And he'll adore you. 
3|0@relationships^1438917715#ctuevv1:solonosolo$:I'm not sure if I'm cool, but thanks. I will try to learn to cook so I can help with meal times. Thanks also for responding. I think this will help me. 
9|0@relationships^1438918807#ctufg24:MissyBear2$:Doesn't matter if you're actually cool or not. I'm quite possibly the biggest dork for 50 miles around but kids love me because I actually give a shit about their interests while being older. You see most adults are fairly dismissive of kids and their interests. They just don't care about the newest pokemon or whatever and don't disguise this "I don't give a shit" very well. So just giving a shit about their interests will give you about a million "cool" points. Also you can drive them places to get icecream. And you'll have awesome stuff they can "borrow". Again, patience is key. Just keep your promises, spend time with them without dismissing them, and take a vague interest in their interests. Just spending 10 minutes while they talk your ear off about pokemon or something else and you'll be their favorite person until they hit puberty during which they'll generally hate everything for a few years. Again- you're going to be fine. And congratulations on being a big brother!
15|0@relationships^1438917416#ctueqeo:dahlialia$:Your sibling is in all likelihood going to idolize you. You will be the 3rd closest person in his/her life, without all the parental authority crap :) I would talk to your mom &amp; Pete. Let them know you are excited but also apprehensive about the changes. You could tell them that you would like to make sure you have one-on-one time with each of them still, after the baby, and if/when they are ready for you to babysit, you could do so and they could go on dates. Ask your mom about you as a baby, and what her experiences were like. That will probably help reassure you too.
8|0@relationships^1438917874#ctueyra:solonosolo$:&gt; Ask your mom about you as a baby, and what her experiences were like. That will probably help reassure you too. Great idea. I think I just need to talk to them and be honest about my feelings, as some people have suggested. I think that will help all of us deal with this situation in a way that makes everyone happy. 
10|0@relationships^1438918016#ctuf19r:LinkAerialSpin$:3rd? Try 1st. You'll the his or her Superman who just does everything perfect without dishing out discipline and punishment. 
