7|0@relationships^1438969501#ctv0pbo:jkh107$:&gt; Fuck no. It will be moody as the day is long. Babies are horrible little monsters who destroy everything they touch. Teenagers destroy only half of what they touch. You win, hands down. Teenagers can articulate what's bothering them. Babies have no choice but to scream until you guess it right. Toddlers are marginally better--they can articulate that they are screaming because they wanted their sandwich cut into triangles and MOM DID RECTANGLES but that doesn't necessarily make them *stop*. Teenagers also take themselves to the bathroom, can help perform complex tasks, can dress themselves, and have many advantages over an infant. I would add that at this age difference, you're probably not going to fight with the baby. That's generally something done by siblings who are much closer in age. The baby is going to get a different type of parental attention than you get. You're going to see them as someone very young (and someone you have to look out for) and s/he will probably see you more as an uncle-type figure than a brother (especially if you go away to school at 18 or move out). 
135|0@relationships^1438915107#ctudgvw:littleorangemonkeys$:It is going to be different, but in a good way. I'm just going to give you a head's up that your mom will be paying more attention to the new baby. It's going to happen, not because she loves the baby *more*, but because babies just need more attention. She gave the same kind of devotion to you when you were that age, but as a teenager, you don't need to have your ass wiped and you can feed yourself, do you own dirty laundry, and heck, even drive yourself places. Do not, I repeat, DO NOT, look at the amount of time she spends on your new sibling as an indication of how much LOVE she has for it vs for you. It's going to save you a lot of heart-ache if you can separate those two things. Time does not equal love. Second, the best way to handle this is to help out a lot. Your mom is going to be tired, the kid is going to cry a lot, and be super messy, and just exhausting, at least for a while. If you help her out by cooking meals, doing chores, changing a diaper or two, you are going to be the knight in shining armor! She's going to look at you and think "Man, OP is the best son in the world, he's so helpful!" Yes, babies are adorable, but they are helpless (and way more moody than a teenager!) and she is going to love you all the more for being the person you are now, someone who can help her out because he's almost an adult. Also, I wouldn't worry too much about your relationship with your new sibling. As a little kid, I was *obsessed* with my uncle, who was 15 years older than me. I thought he was the coolest person ever. I idolized him, and thought it was just the best thing ever to visit him at college. He took me to do all kinds of fun stuff that my parents didn't do. If you are nice to the kid at all, and play with them, I'd say you have at least five solid years of the kid thinking you are their most awesomest friend ever. I would talk to your mom about all of this. She is going to want to know that you feel this way, and if she knows that you're scared about all this stuff, she will be able to make sure you feel included and loved. Maybe you guys have a weekly night just for the two of you, where Pete watches the baby and you get your mom all to yourself again, to catch up. Whatever it takes for you to feel like she is still invested and involved in your life. She can't do anything if she doesn't know you feel this way. 
27|0@relationships^1438915276#ctudkcq:solonosolo$:This was very helpful. Thank you. 
31|0@relationships^1438915496#ctudoyc:NocturnalNightingale$:My mum got pregnant when I was 17 and I felt similar to you. I was a selfish teenager who wanted my mum all to myself.( I was also not an only child at this point, I have another younger sibling, she didn't live with us all the time. )You need to discuss how you're feeling with your mum and Pete. Don't brood and stew over these feelings, because you'll be more likely to lash out or exclude yourself when the new baby does arrive, which doesn't help anyone. Your mother and Pete will not love you any less than the baby, although you may feel that way. They will both be very busy with the baby, babies are a LOT of work., but once again it doesn't mean they love you less. Communicate with them, spend time with them and grow together, don't shut yourself away.
