13|0@relationships^1438916565#ctueauv:NocturnalNightingale$:I guess you adjust with time really, there's no easy answer. My relationship with my brother now is pretty good, even with the age difference. He has a lot of similar interests to me and we go out and do things often. He always seems to want to spend time with me, so be prepared for your sibling to want to be around you too. 
1|0@relationships^1438955577#cturov6:dreadgiveaway$:Every family and situation is different. In my case, I am that baby and there is a ten year age gap. Regarding my siblings, there was a LOT of resentment they had towards me, because my parents saw them as free child care and dumped me on them pretty regularly. I didn't really have a relationship with my siblings until adulthood when we could all talk. That being said, there is the dynamic of the step parent too. This certainly isn't the case for everyone, but there definitely was some sort of "favoritism" towards me, but only in the sense that I was abused slightly less. Awful people will be awful regardless of they are a step parent or not. Same goes for good people. Step families have complicated dynamics. It's worth seeing a therapist for this. All of the feelings you have right now are normal. 
1|0@relationships^1438961400#ctuv98a:LigerXT5$:I'm going to just put this out there. Between my next oldest sibling and I, we are 16 years different. After she moved out, I grew up as an only child, while before she moved out, she was the "only child" for a while, only because we have other siblings. In total, there is six of us. Five of us grew up together, however, I grew up as an only child most of my life. The only concern I can give you, from what my sister has told me, having a younger sibling coming around while around 16 may seem like a terrible thing, it isn't. Other than the side effect of experiencing what it's like to have a baby around, you don't have to worry about the "empty nest" feelings that you're parents go through, if they do, when you move out. The new sibling will have to put up with that when they move out! You may lose some freedom here and there, but you gain others, but as far as I can say, that varies from family to family, situation to situation. The fear you are experiencing is of the unknown, which happens to us all. Just move through it, learn and experience what you can, and you'll do just fine.
-1|0@relationships^1438964010#ctux0jn:over20$:It's perfectly normal, and the feelings may never go away completely in some form. It may be permanent "sibling rivalry". But that's how life is. If women only had one child, our population would go down by half every generation, even if every woman had a child. I was an only child, and I remember there was an age where I was glad of that, though my parents might have been quite happy to have more. But it was out of my hands, and anyway now I sort of wish I did have siblings. Finally, at 16 she's about done raising you. You may feel very incomplete and insecure, but it's getting to be time for you to take over and go on your own. Soon you'll be keeping girlfriends or a wife company and won't have much time for mom anyway.
1|0@relationships^1438968553#ctv0236:aberl$:&gt;What is it she thinks she'll get by having another child? If I'm a good kid like she says, then why does she need another child? Well, the facts are that a 40-year-old woman with a teenager and no younger children, who has a boyfriend not a husband, is probably not trying to get pregnant. You're old enough to ask her outright if you really want to know, but if you don't want to ask it's pretty safe to assume that mom &amp; boyfriend are having an "oops" baby. It's life, they're rolling with it, nothing at all to do with replacing you.
